/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A tyranny makes its population uneducated, mistrust one another, and incapable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The tyrant, in order to maintain his power, tries to make the population</p><ul><li>uneducated, unambitious, and parochial, </li><li>lose trust in others, and </li><li>incapable of action against the tyrant, i.e., keeping them impoverished or overworked or under crushing taxation.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>These are the three things which Aristotle identifies that tyrants do in order to maintain power, and they remain true today.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, the second point is something that is true by the very nature of common knowledges, since what is common knowledge is something that people don’t always know, and if the punishments are strict enough and people mistrust each other enough, they will not break free of chains of the state for the fear that others might not feel the same. Aristotle also identifies some other characteristics of tyrants:</p><ul><li>That they are warmongers, so that a strong leader always seems needed,</li><li>That they set the rich against the poor and highlight other divisions,</li><li>That they enforce heavy surveillance, and</li><li>That they prevent people some associating freely.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a><ul><li>It seems that through education, the state is able to assert some of the control highlighted here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>It could be shaped wrongly in a tyrannical regime.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check\">Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check</a><ul><li>Maybe taxes are not always effective as a means of suppression?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rentier states typically have high inequality and low political participation\">Rentier states typically have high inequality and low political participation</a><ul><li>Rentier states are often tyrannical too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division\">Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division</a><ul><li>This is related to the point of tyrants being warmongering.</li></ul></li></ul>","A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A “torn country” is one where there are competing dominant culture groups in a nation. There are several steps that may be taken in order to redefine a nation’s culture.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>There are many examples of torn countries in the world, and this note gives a way that they may be able to redefine themselves</p><h4>Torn Countries</h4><p>Torn countries are able to redefine their culture, given that they meet the following three criteria:</p><ol><li>Support from the political and economic elite of the country.</li><li>Public acquiescence (not necessarily support) towards the shift.</li><li>The dominant elements in the host civilizations must be receptive to the new civilization joining.</li></ol><h4>Examples of Torn Countries</h4><p><strong>Russia</strong></p><ul><li>During the Soviet Era, they were united by their ideology and the fact that they were different from the West because they were more advanced.</li><li>Today, there are divisions in the nation between the “nationalists” and the “Atlantists,” the former arguing that Russia should turn its focus to the East/South, and the latter arguing that Russia should turn to the West.</li></ul><p><strong>Turkey</strong></p><ul><li>Ataturk was able to Westernize and modernize the nation.</li><li>Increasingly, there is a tide of shifting towards Islamization.<ul><li>This is partly because when Turkey tried to join Western civilization (the EU), they were rejected since the West was unable to accept the cultural different between them and the Muslim Turks.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Mexico</strong></p><ul><li>It was economically liberalized by Salinas in the 1980s.</li><li>It represented a struggle between North American and Latin American cultures.<ul><li>It has not been able to completely North Americanize itself due to resistance from the general public.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Australia</strong></p><ul><li>It was historically Western.</li><li>Today, it has expressed a desire to join Asia. This is impossible, since:<ul><li>Both its elite and general population are either apathetic about the idea or oppose it.</li><li>Other Asian countries oppose it on the grounds of a cultural difference.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture\">Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture</a><ul><li>Australia has tried to shift its culture to that of Asia, but this has not been possible despite increasing trade ties between the two civilizations.</li></ul></li></ul>","All things are one and the same":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core principle of Hui Shi’s (惠施) branch of the School of Names, the idea that since all differences between things are merely relative, we cannot objective state there to be any differences between things, and thus, we must love all things equally. A similar concept of <em>qi</em> appears in Neo-Confucianist thought.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was one the main schools of the School of Names and the Neo-Confucianist school.</p><h4>School of Names</h4><p>Hui Shi gives the following example that what is big must be big in relation to something else, and what is small must be small in relation to something else. Therefore, we can say that a mountain and a grain of sand are the same size, since relative to the massiveness of the cosmos, they are both insignificant.</p><h4>Neo-Confucianism</h4><p>Their understanding, explicates by people such as Zhang Zai (张载) of the <strong>School of Principle</strong>, is that the universe is made of <em>qi</em>. Therefore, since all things are made of one underlying substance, this metaphysical unity must constitute a commensurate moral unity. Since everything is ultimately “part of one body,” harming any part of the body is equivalent to harming the self. The concept of 生无所得 is likewise related to this. He argues that there is two types of <em>qi</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Dispersed</strong>: this manifests as the formless Great Void.</li><li><strong>Condensed</strong>: these are the particular things in the world, including humans.</li></ul><p>When we die, we merely transform from the latter to the former. In life, we follow and serve as per the Confucian doctrine, and then we simply die.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">《西铭》（亦作《正蒙·乾称篇》）</p><p>存，吾顺事；没，吾宁也。 In life I follow and serve; in death I am at peace.</p></blockquote><p>The School of Mind claims unity in the world rather through the concept that the <em>li</em> is something that is universal and in fact permeates all of existence. At its deepest level, the same <em>li</em> that constitutes one’s innate moral nature is the universal pattern/order of reality; this is the reason that one’s spontaneous moral concern can extend outward.</p><ul><li>The sage is the one who is ultimately able to understand the unity of self and the rest of reality: realizing metaphysical unity produces benevolence.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a><ul><li>In this way, the School of Names derives the same principle through a different argument.</li></ul></li></ul>","Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As the Mongols originated in the plains of Northern China, where the temperature fluctuates widely (-30 C to 40 C), they were adapted to fight in many different areas around the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the Mongols were able to take such a large amount of land, and also shows that conditioning oneself to a variety of situations allows oneself to perform better when those situations inevitably arrive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200\">Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200</a><ul><li>A second climatic factor, though one acting through horse supply rather than through adaptation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe\">The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe</a><ul><li>The social form that this environment selected for.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered\">The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered</a><ul><li>Again demonstrates their adaptability.</li></ul></li></ul>","Capitalism undermines democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A common claim among leftists is that capitalism fundamentally erodes our ability to peacefully cooperate, and the greed associated with capitalism engenders inequality and class division which eventually leads to corrupt government and the end of democracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common claim made by leftists.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>Essentially, the things described in this note is what contributes to the current note’s claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a><ul><li>Many identify this as a core issue.</li></ul></li></ul>","China will dominate in East Asia":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that China will be a regional, if not global power through its domination of East Asia.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has implications for US foreign policy towards China.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is fundamentally derived through the regression towards the mean, as China has historically been dominant in East Asia, and has only become weak after its contact with the West. China is predicted to regress towards the mean, i.e., the state whereby China is the dominant power in East Asia.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>China is likely to enforce this dominance through non-expansionary means.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The around 60% of China’s total trade goes through the Malacca Strait\">The around 60% of China’s total trade goes through the Malacca Strait</a><ul><li>This could potentially be a barrier to China’s domination.</li></ul></li></ul>","China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>China’s basic social contract is one which gives the state dictatorial powers, but the people positive rights and economic development.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding the way that the Chinese government works, and the foundations that underlie the Chinese state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>This idea is again present here, as China’s strong state is predicated on the fact that they provide positive rights for their citizens.</li></ul></li></ul>","China's foreign policy is characterized by 'theatrical diplomacy'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>China’s foreign policy approach is marked by their “theatrical diplomacy,” where they engage in essentially risk-free diplomatic gestures which engender other countries’ goodwill towards China.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains an important aspect of China’s foreign policy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“China Gains Favor With Trump by Dipping Into Iran Diplomacy”</p><p>Underneath its peace proposal lies what some call a “China First” policy—a cautious strategy that gives priority to symbolic wins and never assumes significant risk for the sake of global stability. This “theatrical diplomacy” illustrates the limits of Beijing’s influence even as it casts itself as a superpower peer to the U.S. “The prevailing mindset in Beijing is that China is not sticking its neck out for anybody,” said Danny Russel, a former senior U.S. diplomat now with the Asia Society Policy Institute. “China will only engage in mediation that allows it to claim credit without incurring any real risk or cost.“”</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China will dominate in East Asia\">China will dominate in East Asia</a><ul><li>Perhaps part of the way that China will be able to dominate is through a soft power approach aided by this theatrical diplomacy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>Related concept to the idea in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A world dominated by China will be radically different from a world dominated by the US\">A world dominated by China will be radically different from a world dominated by the US</a><ul><li>Extending the logic of this note, a world dominated by China would be one with less public goods provided, which is exactly what this note explains.</li></ul></li></ul>","Human social relations are non-trivial":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that human social relations are non-trivial and go beyond mere prudential concerns.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This builds upon the contractarian moral framework by appending the consideration that people’s relation with each other are non-trivial.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In order to ultimately reach one’s full potential, a community is required. In other words, the whole community together outweighs each individual by themselves added together. There are two sub-claims related to this idea:</p><ol><li>That successful, just institutions are the shared and final end.</li><li>That such institutions are prized as goods in themselves, rather than merely instrumentally.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>This is an example of non-trivial social relations.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that humans have rapidly grown to be able to wield advanced technologies, while our sense of how to use them lags behind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This proses a significant challenge, since it means that while we yield significantly more power than before, we still do not realize the how to use this power effectively and responsibly.</p><h4>References</h4>","In a democracy, the adjudicative power and the executive power should not be vested in the same person":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a democracy, one must be able to separate the adjudicative and the executive powers, since allowing the same person to hold both positions gives them too much power, and decentralizing the power in this way means that competing incentives between the two people holding respective positions weakens the influence of both.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle mentioned this as being especially important when it comes to assigning offices in a democracy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This can manifest in the practice of makes the multitude the deliberative body (or having people from this group hold the power of deliberation) while having the notables, i.e., the virtuous, be the executive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Power corrupts those who possess it\">Power corrupts those who possess it</a><ul><li>When a person has too much power, as here, it ends badly for others/their enemies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>Some of the key intermolecular forces include hydrogen bonds and Van der Waals interactions.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>Intermolecular forces are important since they play an inportmant role in biology, for example, in the formation of amino acids.</p><h4>Forces</h4><ul><li><strong>Hydrogen bonds</strong>: a result of partial chrages of polar molecules.</li><li><strong>Van der Waals</strong>: a weak attractive force that occurs between all molecules.</li></ul><p>Hydrogen bonds allow for the formation of secondary structures, as the various R-structures of polypeptides interact with each other to create $\\alpha$ -helices and $\\beta$ -sheets. They are most prominent in non-polar molecules, since such molecules have no other interactions, but are present in all molecular interactions.</p>","Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Following the fall of the Srivijaya Empire in the 13th century, Muslim merchants and proselytizer were able to convert much of the areas former Buddhism into Islam.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason that Islam is so prevalent in Southeast Asia.</p><h4>References</h4>","Issues involving justice involve high levels of utility":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Claims of justice and injustice are especially weighty, utility-wise, compared to other, non-justice claims. In other words, they are incommensurate with other issues of mere expediency.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This makes justice <em>sui genris</em> as an utilitarian phenomenon, and thus requiring special treatment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This idea by Mill is not claiming that there is some lexical priority which must be given to the principles of justice, as Rawls does. Rather, he merely claims that, <em>in general,</em> claims regarding justice are heavier ethical claims, which means that they would then be considered beyond matters of mere expediency.</p><h4>References</h4>","Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice as fairness only applies to institutions, and does not apply to individual dealings.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial caveat to the application of justice as fairness, namely, that it not cover the aspects of life that are not voluntary.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice as fairness is just a theory of the basic structure of society, and has a uniquely pervasive and non-voluntary influence on individuals’ life prospects. Additionally, applying this principle to individuals on every case would be too demanding.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony\">Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony</a><ul><li>This is more possible given that the focus of justice is regarding institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>Aristotle also relates justice to state institutions, specifically, the laws of a state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>Is justice impossible for institutions?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This would be the an example of an institutions that is in need of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moral truths arise internally and naturally":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Moral truths do not originate from some metaphysical nature outside of oneself. Rather, it arises naturally out of physical substance of our minds.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the view towards the nature of morality for the School of Mind, a branch of Neo-Confucianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Crucially, the School of Mind breakdown the division between <em>li</em> and <em>qi</em> such that there is no longer a clear metaphysical separation as in the School of Principle. Rather, the heart-mind (心) is the <em>li</em>.</p><ul><li><em>Li</em>, therefore, is a characterization of the heart-mind when it responds accordingly to circumstances as dictated by the <em>liangzhi</em>.</li></ul><p>The ultimate goal of the School of Mind, therefore, is to be able to act without external motivation and purely according to the innate and infallible <em>liangzhi</em>. In doing so, one begins to understand the <em>li</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things are one and the same\">All things are one and the same</a><ul><li>This is the fundamental principle that underlies the thought of the School of Mind.</li></ul></li></ul>","Naval strategy can seek either decisive battle or the disruption of enemy communication":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While Mahan pursues the elimination of the enemy fleet through decisive battle, thus establishing absolute naval control, Corbett argues for the disruption of enemy supply and communication lines without decisive battle in an attempt to establish relative naval control.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This contrasts the difference between Mahanian and Corbettian naval strategies, as while one focuses on complete and absolute control, the other focuses on disrupting the ability of the enemy to wage war more indirectly.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A limited war is best fought on the strategic defensive and the operational offensive\">A limited war is best fought on the strategic defensive and the operational offensive</a><ul><li>A related idea of Corbett.</li></ul></li></ul>","Neo-Confucianism was Confucianism influenced by Buddhism and Daoism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Neo-Confucianism (宋明理学) was an attempt to create a more rationalist version of Confucianism, free from the influence of Daoism and Buddhism. At the same time, it incorporated many elements of Daoist and Buddhist vocabulary.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Neo-Confucianism’s development in the Tang and Song dynasty was an attempt to rid Confucianism of its Daoist and Buddhist roots, and at the same time, revive and reinvigorate an old tradition.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs\">Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs</a><ul><li>The core ideas of Daoism that influenced the Neo-Confucian revival.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">The Yin-Yang symbol represents the duality and synthesis between Daoism and Confucianism</span><ul><li>Perhaps this relates to the Neo-Confucian revival?</li></ul></li></ul>","Plate 9 from 'The Disasters of War', or 'They don't like to'":"<h4>Addendum</h4><h4>References</h4>","Pleasure is not solely the Good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pleasure is not the sole thing that determines whether something is good or not. Rather, it can enhance/contribute to goodness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that we should still strive for pleasure, although it is not our sole aim in life.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The Good should be something that is determinant and unchanging, while pleasure could be had as a result of different activities and indifferent degrees.</li><li>Additionally, there are something that don’t necessarily bring pleasure that it would be good to have anyways, even if we have pleasure. This means that pleasure cannot be the sole thing that is good.</li><li>Also, not every form of pleasure is desirable.<ul><li>Bodily pleasure in the excess, for instance, is not good.</li><li>Also, Alien pleasure is not good, and are in fact analogous to pain, since they distract us from being able to experience the proper pleasure in a situation.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life\">Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life</a><ul><li>What pleasure is.</li></ul></li></ul>","Producers capture almost none of the surplus generated through innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Producers capture very little of the surplus that is generated through innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the difficulty of innovation in a market capitalist system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Only about 2.2% of total social surplus is captured by innovators.</li><li>The rest of the 98% actually goes to the consumer in the form of lower prices, better products, and higher standards of living.</li><li>Markets typically overestimate Schumpeterian profits and underestimate the decay of such profit.<ul><li>Technology has increased the decay speed, since information is difficult to create, but easy to re-create.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning, whereas most models use SFT":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning to optimize the reasoning process, in addition to being first trained using supervised pretraining. On the other hand, normal models also first train with the supervised pretraining, then they are  subject to reinforcement learning, but simply to optimize their outputs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This elucidates the difference between reasoning and normal models.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Reasoning LLMs, in their post-training phase, focus on optimizing their reasoning process through RLHF (reinforcement learning from human feedback) or RLAIF (reinforcement learning from AI feedback). On the other hand, normal models will simply have their outputs optimized with the same tools.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Frankfurt cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (PAP).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The PAP is a core tenet of libertarian schools of thought. If it is invalidated, then the libertarian school of thought will be damaged.</p><h4>Argument</h4><ul><li><strong>The Counterexample:</strong> Frankfurt describes a scenario where a neurosurgeon implants a chip in your brain. If you try to vote for Party A, the chip forces you to vote for Party B. If you choose to vote for Party B on your own, the chip does nothing.</li><li><strong>The Result:</strong> You voted for Party B on your own. You are responsible. Yet, because of the chip, you <em>could not have done otherwise</em>. This suggests freedom depends on the <em>source</em> of the action, not the options available.</li></ul><h4>Consequences</h4><p>Two conclusions follow if the cases work. The ability to do otherwise is not necessary for moral responsibility, and freedom is a property of the actual sequence rather than of the alternatives that were available, so what did not happen is irrelevant to whether the agent is responsible.</p><h4>The Dilemma Objection</h4><p>The standard reply is that the cases face a dilemma. If the agent’s decision is determined, the incompatibilist simply denies responsibility and the case begs the question. If it is undetermined, then the agent could have decided otherwise after all, and the alternative has not been removed. Either way the case fails to show what it claims.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility\">Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility</a><ul><li>Why the cases target responsibility rather than freedom directly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past\">The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past</a><ul><li>The incompatibilist argument these cases are meant to sidestep.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The structure of the government of the Roman Republic was designed in such a way as to balance the power of its various elements, so that the government is more structurally sound.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the efficiency and power of the Roman Republican government.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The three parts of the government: the Senate, the Consuls, and the people all balance each other, since they each hold powers which check the others’ power.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>When any one branch of the three parts becomes too powerful, its power is checked by the other two branches.</li></ul></li></ul>","The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The first wave of democracy began around the 1820, and ended around the 1920s.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the longest and arguably, most impactful wave of democratization. It is also the one that had the most conspicuous reversal, with the rise of Fascism and Communism at its end.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The first wave of democracy began around the 1820s, when a majority of white males in the US were able to vote. Over the next decades, countries around the world began to democratize. This wave ended around 1922, when Benito Mussolini overturned the democratic government in Italy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals\">The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>Some draw a similarity to today.</li></ul></li></ul>","We shouldn’t calculate utility before each action, but rather construct rules for generally beneficial actions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fundamental idea of rule utilitarianism, where utility is not calculate before each action, but rather a set of generally utility improving actions are drawn up, and people merely act in accordance with them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main branches of utilitarianism.</p><h4>References</h4>"});
